/* ============================================================================
octal-to-decimal.epher -- octal literals read as decimal values

Evaluates octal literals and prints their decimal values. An octal
literal is the prefix 0o followed by digits 0 to 7, and its value is
the sum of the powers of eight in it: 0o17 = 8 + 7 = 15. The prefix
changes the spelling, never the value, so the results below are plain
decimal numbers.

Source: standard numeral systems; positional notation in base 8, the
place values 1, 8, 64 and 512 made explicit under the first check.
Evaluation is exact for every value here.

Run it from a terminal:
  Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/number-theory/bases/octal-to-decimal.epher
  Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\number-theory\bases\octal-to-decimal.epher"
  macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/number-theory/bases/octal-to-decimal.epher
In the REPL:             load one of the paths above
Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter

There is no knob: the sample literals are the point of the script.
============================================================================ */

// ---- the place values -----------------------------------------------------------------
// 0o777 has 7 in the 64, 8 and 1 places, so it is 7 * 64 + 7 * 8 + 7.
print("7 * 64 + 7 * 8 + 7:", 7 * 64 + 7 * 8 + 7)
0o17                          // one eight and seven ones: 15
0o777                         // the largest three-digit octal: 511
0o1000                        // 8^3 = 512
0o52                          // five eights and two ones: 42

// ---- a list at a glance ---------------------------------------------------------------
for x in {0o1, 0o7, 0o10, 0o17, 0o52, 0o100, 0o777} do x
/* ---- expected output ----
= 7 * 64 + 7 * 8 + 7: 511
= 15
= 511
= 512
= 42
= {1, 7, 8, 15, 42, 64, 511}
*/
